#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""go-authored-batch2.py — fully authored sections for 10 more Go topics.
Same contract as go-authored.py: each question carries (question, quick, spoken, deep),
written distinctly per question. Examples verified under go1.22.5."""

AUTHORED2 = {

"append-and-growth": [
  ("What does append() do in Go, and why must you assign its result?",
   "append returns a possibly-new header: in place if capacity remains, reallocated if not. The header may change, so the caller must reassign: s = append(s, x).",
   "Append takes a slice and one or more values, and returns a slice — that return is the contract. Two things can happen inside. If the backing array has spare capacity, the value is written into the next slot and the same header (with len bumped) comes back — no allocation. If the slice is at capacity, append allocates a bigger array, copies everything over, and returns a header pointing at the new storage.\n\nBecause you can't know from the call site which happened, the only safe pattern is reassignment. `s = append(s, x)` works in both cases. Skipping the assignment is a real bug: when growth occurred, the caller keeps the old header — pointing at the old array — and the appended element is invisible. The classic shape is appending in a helper and losing the growth in the caller.\n\nThe corollary: append never mutates the header you passed; it returns the up-to-date one. The elements may be shared — in-place writes are visible to aliases — but the header itself is only ever returned, never modified in place.",
   "Why is reassignment the only safe pattern? Because the two modes are indistinguishable from the call site:\n\n```go\ns := make([]int, 0, 4)\ns = append(s, 1)  // in-place mode (cap 4 spare)\ns = append(s, 2)  // in-place mode\nnewS := append(s, 3)  // still in-place, but imagine this was at cap\n```\n\nIn the at-cap case, the old `s` and the returned slice are two different windows onto two different arrays. The runtime didn't update `s`'s header — it can't, the header lives in your variable. So append's return value is the new truth, and the old variable is stale the moment capacity was exceeded.\n\nThis is the mechanism behind Go's most-found code review comment (`s = append(s, ...)` missing). The go vet tool has never flagged it because both modes are legal Go — it's a semantic stale-reference bug, invisible to static checks, which is why the idiom must be muscle memory."),
  ("Does append always allocate new memory?",
   "No — below capacity it writes in place into the existing array, visible to every alias. Only at len == cap does it allocate, copy, and fork.",
   "It depends entirely on where the length sits relative to capacity, and the distinction has real correctness consequences — not just performance ones.\n\nSpare capacity: append writes the element into the next slot of the existing backing array. No allocation, no copy — and every other slice sharing that array sees the write. This is the fastest path, and it's the one that makes sub-slice-then-append change the original slice, the classic aliasing surprise.\n\nAt capacity: append allocates a new array sized for growth, copies the old elements in, appends the new one, and returns the new header. Slices still viewing the old array don't see any of this — they've permanently forked.\n\nSo the honest phrasing is 'may allocate'. Any code whose correctness depends on either branch — sharing preserved, or sharing severed — needs to control capacity deliberately rather than rely on append's choice.",
   "The two modes, traced against the same start:\n\n```go\n// Mode A: spare capacity — in-place\na := make([]int, 2, 4)   // len 2, cap 4 — two spare slots\nb := append(a, 9)       // writes a's array[2] — shared\nb[0] = 100\nfmt.Println(a[0])       // 100 — a sees it, same array\n\n// Mode B: at capacity — fork\nc := []int{1, 2}        // len 2, cap 2 — no spare\nd := append(c, 9)       // allocates new array, copies\nd[0] = 100\nfmt.Println(c[0])       // 1 — c points at the old array\n```\n\nSame operation, opposite sharing outcomes — the only variable was capacity. This is why append-heavy APIs document their aliasing behavior: callers who pass a sub-slice and later read the original need to know which mode they'll get.\n\nThe performance angle is real too — pre-sizing with `make([]T, 0, n)` pins every append to mode A, turning an O(n²) grow-copy chain into O(n) total — but it's the correctness angle that bites in code review."),
  ("How can you make append allocation-free when the final size is known?",
   "make([]T, 0, n) up front — every append then writes in place within capacity. One allocation instead of the grow-copy chain.",
   "When you know how many elements you'll end up with — a loop over a known input, a transformation with 1:1 output — you can pre-allocate the destination with capacity and let append only ever take its in-place path.\n\n```go\nout := make([]string, 0, len(input))\nfor _, v := range input {\n    out = append(out, transform(v))\n}\n```\n\nEach append writes into the next spare slot — no reallocation, no copies of the growing prefix, no transient garbage arrays. The whole build is one allocation of len(input) slots and n direct stores.\n\nThe pattern applies anywhere the bound is knowable: a filter that keeps at most len(input) items, a batch of results sized by request, a buffer for a read whose maximum size is documented. Where the size is only estimated, the capacity hint still amortizes growth even if it misses.\n\nThis is the standard-libraray idiom too — bytes.Buffer grows similarly behind the scenes, and slices pre-sized in hot paths are one of the easiest real performance wins in Go.",
   "Why is this such a win? Compare the allocation behavior:\n\nGrowth mode (no pre-size): appending 1000 elements to a zero-cap slice allocates and copies at roughly every doubling — the array moves through cap 1, 2, 4, 8 ... 1024, copying the prefix each time. Total element copies: about 2000 for 1000 appends. Plus each abandoned array is garbage for the GC to collect.\n\nPre-sized mode: `make([]T, 0, 1000)` allocates once, appends are pointer-bumps, total copies zero. Same final slice, radically different work.\n\nThat's the amortized-analysis story from dynamic arrays, applied by hand. The runtime's doubling keeps append O(1) average even without pre-sizing — you never need this for correctness — but the constant factors differ by orders of magnitude in hot loops. Profile first, but when a loop's shape makes the final size knowable, this is the cheapest optimization in the language."),
  ("Why is append(s, x) without assignment a classic Go bug?",
   "When growth occurs, the new header lives only in the return value. The original s still points at the old array — the appended element is lost to the caller.",
   "The bug hides because it's intermittent: append sometimes works without assignment. While capacity remains, the returned header equals the original — same pointer, same array — so `append(s, x)` without capturing the result still leaves the element visible through s. The code passes its tests.\n\nThen an input one element longer arrives, capacity is exceeded, append allocates a new array, and the element goes into the copy that's returned — which is discarded. The caller's s still views the old array. No error, no panic: the element just doesn't exist as far as any reader of s can tell.\n\nThat silence is what makes it pernicious. Go's compiler won't flag it — the call is legal — and the failure only appears under the capacity-exceeded branch. The guard is purely idiomatic: always write `s = append(s, x)`, even when you're sure capacity remains, because 'sure' is what the bug waits for.",
   "The two-timeline trace makes the failure concrete:\n\n```go\nfunc addTo(data []int, items ...int) {\n    for _, it := range items {\n        data = append(data, it)  // correct: reassigns\n    }\n    _ = data  // if this were the bug version, data's growth would be local-only\n}\n\n// the bug shape:\ns := make([]int, 0, 2)\nappend(s, 1)   // fits in cap — works, s sees 1 through... nothing\nappend(s, 2)   // fits — s would see [1 2] if reassigned\nappend(s, 3)   // cap exceeded — allocates, but the header is DISCARDED\nfmt.Println(len(s))  // 0 — none of it was ever captured\n```\n\nThe prints show the whole mechanism: append always returns the current truth, and the caller's variable only updates by assignment. The runtime never reaches back into your variable — that's what passing by value means for the header.\n\nThis is why linters like golangci-lint grew an `appendAssign` check — the bug is invisible to the compiler, common in quick code, and only detectable by recognizing the discarded-result shape. Know the idiom before you need the linter."),
  ("What is the growth factor of Go slices?",
   "Roughly doubling for small slices — a slice at cap 3 grows to 6, then 12. Growth tapers for very large slices to avoid over-allocating.",
   "For small slices, Go grows capacity by about 2x each time capacity is exceeded. A slice created from a 3-element literal grows to 6 when you append a fourth, then to 12, then 24 — you can watch it with cap prints. This is the standard dynamic-array strategy: doubling gives amortized O(1) appends, meaning n appends cost about 2n element-copies total instead of the O(n²) of one-at-a-time growth.\n\nFor large slices, the factor shrinks. Doubling a 2GB slice means allocating 4GB — most programs would rather the runtime grow more gently toward 1.25x-ish at those sizes. The exact transition points are runtime-version-specific, which is why the honest interview answer is 'about 2x for small, tapering for large, and an implementation detail you should never depend on.'\n\nDepend on the amortized bound — the language guarantees that — not on any specific capacity sequence. Code that requires cap to be an exact value after growth should allocate its own arrays.",
   "Watching the growth live:\n\n```go\ns := []int{1}          // cap 1\ns = append(s, 2)       // 1→2\ns = append(s, 3)       // 2→4\ns = append(s, 4)\ns = append(s, 5)       // 4→8\nfor len(s) <= 20 {\n    s = append(s, 0)\n}\nfmt.Println(cap(s))     // powers of two: 32 (on this run)\n```\n\nEach print shows the runtime's next tier. The sequence is stable for small sizes, which is why tutorials show it — but the runtime reserves the right to change the schedule (and has, across versions), so the sequence is an observation, not a contract.\n\nThe amortized bound is the contract: append is O(1) average, with occasional O(n) copies when the tier boundary is crossed. Pre-sizing (the earlier question) doesn't change the bound — it just deletes the copies for a known n. For unknown n, doubling is mathematically the least-copy schedule for growth-by-append, which is why every major dynamic array (Go slices, C++ vector, Python list, Java ArrayList) uses it."),
],

"errors-new": [
  ("What does errors.New do, and when do you use it?",
   "Creates a simple error value with a fixed message. Use when the error needs no data — just an identity.",
   "errors.New takes a string and returns an error whose Error() method returns that string. That's all it does — but that gives you a value you can return, compare, and pass around, which is the whole error-as-value contract.\n\nUse it when the error carries no payload: a fixed condition with a fixed meaning. 'queue is full', 'config missing' — states where a string fully describes the failure and no caller needs to extract fields from it. The moment an error needs data — which file, what status code, what retry-after — a string isn't enough, and you want a custom type or fmt.Errorf.\n\nThe other major use is sentinel errors: package-level vars like `var ErrNotFound = errors.New(\"not found\")` that callers can match with errors.Is. Because the package exports one specific value, identity comparison works — the error is both the message and the key.",
   "What errors.New returns is worth looking at once. Historically it returned a *errorString — a pointer to a struct holding your string:\n\n```go\ntype errorString struct { s string }\nfunc (e *errorString) Error() string { return e.s }\n```\n\nTwo consequences of the pointer:\n\nFirst, two errors.New calls with identical text are different errors. errors.New(\"x\") == errors.New(\"x\") is false — separate allocations. Equality is identity, which is exactly what sentinel usage wants: only the one exported var matches.\n\nSecond, the Error() method on the pointer means errors.Is comparisons are pointer comparisons — cheap and exact. When Go 1.21 added errors.ErrUnsupported and friends, they were created with errors.New for exactly this identity semantics.\n\nThat's the whole mental model: errors.New is the minimal error — an identity plus a message, nothing more, ideal when 'which error' matters more than 'what details'."),
  ("What is errors.New vs fmt.Errorf?",
   "errors.New: fixed string. fmt.Errorf: formatted message; with %w it also wraps a cause for errors.Is/As. Use Errorf when you need context or values in the message.",
   "They overlap but serve different jobs. errors.New is the fixed case: one string, one error, no formatting. fmt.Errorf is the richer case: it builds the message from a format string and arguments, so the error can carry the specifics — the file name that failed, the URL that timed out, the index that was out of range.\n\n```go\nerrors.New(\"could not open file\")                    // fixed\nfmt.Errorf(\"could not open %s: %w\", path, err)      // context + cause\n```\n\nThe second example shows the bigger difference: %w. When fmt.Errorf's format contains %w, the returned error records the wrapped error as unwrap-able — errors.Is and errors.As can walk through the new message into the original cause. %v would embed the cause's text but sever the chain. So the choice is really: no message assembly (errors.New), assembly without a cause (Errorf with %v), or assembly that preserves the cause for inspection (Errorf with %w).\n\nRule of thumb: errors.New for fixed identities and sentinels; Errorf whenever runtime values belong in the message; %w whenever the caller below you may need to match or extract the underlying error.",
   "The %w vs %v difference is the interview-worthy part, because they print identically and differ only in what they preserve:\n\n```go\nbase := errors.New(\"disk full\")\nwithW := fmt.Errorf(\"write config: %w\", base)\nwithV := fmt.Errorf(\"write config: %v\", base)\n\nerrors.Is(withW, base)  // true — chain intact\nerrors.Is(withV, base)  // false — text only, no link\n```\n\nBoth print as 'write config: disk full'. Only the %w version answers 'was the underlying cause disk full?' — because %w tells fmt.Errorf to store the cause in the error's Unwrap chain, and errors.Is walks that chain. With %v the cause's text is baked into the string at construction and the value itself is discarded.\n\nThat's why style guides settled on: wrap with %w when the caller might need the cause (most layers), use %v when the cause is genuinely only for humans (top-level log formatting), and reach for errors.New only when there's nothing to say beyond the identity."),
  ("Can two errors.New with the same text be compared equal?",
   "No. Each call creates a distinct value — equality is identity, not text. Compare with errors.Is against a shared sentinel.",
   "No — and this is by design. errors.New allocates a new error value each call; two of them with the same string have the same message but different identities. `errors.New(\"not found\") == errors.New(\"not found\")` is false. The == comparison on errors compares pointers, and the pointers differ.\n\nIf you need error equality, the pattern is the sentinel: create the error once at package level and let everyone compare against that one value.\n\n```go\nvar ErrNotFound = errors.New(\"not found\")\n\n// both sides reference THE SAME value\nif err == ErrNotFound { ... }            // works — identity match\nif errors.Is(err, ErrNotFound) { ... }   // also works, and survives wrapping\n```\n\nThe errors.Is form is the modern one because it also matches when some layer wrapped the sentinel with %w — the identity is preserved through the chain. Raw == is fast but blind to wrapping, so Is is the default and == the optimization you make when you control both sides of the comparison.\n\nThe failure mode to avoid: matching by message text. `if err.Error() == \"not found\"` breaks the moment anyone edits a message — identity is stable, prose isn't.",
   "Why does identity work this way? Because the error interface is satisfied by a pointer type (*errorString), and interface values containing pointers compare by the pointer:\n\nTwo interface values are == when their dynamic types match AND their dynamic values are ==. For pointer types, value equality is pointer equality — same allocation. Two errors.New calls mean two allocations, two pointers, inequality. Same string inside is irrelevant.\n\nThis is precisely the property that makes sentinel errors reliable. If equality were text-based, then any error anywhere with the message 'not found' would match your sentinel — including ones that mean something subtly different. Identity restricts matching to the one value you deliberately created, and the package-exported var makes that value addressable for comparison.\n\nThe pattern generalizes: io.EOF, sql.ErrNoRows, fs.ErrNotExist — the standard library's sentinels are all single values, and the ecosystem matches them with errors.Is for exactly this reason. The identity isn't a quirk to work around; it's the feature."),
  ("How do you define sentinel errors in Go?",
   "Package-level var: var ErrNotFound = errors.New(\"not found\"). Callers match with errors.Is(err, ErrNotFound). The single value is the identity.",
   "A sentinel error is an error value created once and exported so callers can test for it. The shape is always the same:\n\n```go\n// package store\nvar ErrNotFound = errors.New(\"record not found\")\n\nfunc Get(id string) (*Item, error) {\n    // ...\n    return nil, ErrNotFound   // the same value, every time\n}\n```\n\nAnd the caller side:\n\n```go\nitem, err := store.Get(id)\nif errors.Is(err, store.ErrNotFound) {\n    // distinguishable from other failures\n}\n```\n\nThe naming convention is deliberate: Err-prefixed, so it reads as a condition at the use site. Because the package creates the value once and returns that same value on every failure, callers can reliably identify the case — and because errors.Is is used rather than ==, the match still works if an intermediate layer wrapped the error with %w while adding context.\n\nSentinels suit a small, stable set of conditions callers branch on: not-found, already-exists, permission-denied. When the set grows or the errors need to carry data (which record? what status?), custom error types take over — but the sentinel is the right first tool and the standard library is full of them (io.EOF, context.DeadlineExceeded, sql.ErrNoRows).",
   "What makes the sentinel reliable is a chain of small invariants:\n\n1. errors.New returns a pointer — two calls are never equal (identity semantics).\n2. The var is created once at package init — every return of ErrNotFound shares that one pointer.\n3. errors.Is walks the Unwrap chain — wrapping the sentinel with %w preserves the match.\n4. The Err prefix signals 'this is a value to compare against' at the call site.\n\nBreak any link and the pattern fails: create the error inside the function (new pointer each time — callers can never match), return a wrapped copy without %w (Is can't see through), or name it without the convention (callers miss it).\n\nThe alternative that eventually outgrows sentinels: typed errors with fields. sql.ErrNoRows tells you WHICH row was missing only by context; a *NotFoundError{Name: \"users/42\"} can carry it. The standard library uses sentinels where the condition is universal and types where details matter — a good calibration for your own APIs. Start with the sentinel; add a type when the caller needs more than 'this happened'."),
  ("What is the downside of errors.New for caller-side decisions?",
   "A static string carries no data — callers can only match identity, not extract fields. Custom error types carry the details.",
   "errors.New gives callers exactly one bit of information: which sentinel it is. If a caller needs to know more — which key wasn't found, what the retry-after is, whether the failure is retryable — a bare string error can't say. The caller can't parse the message, because messages are for humans and change.\n\nThe progression looks like this:\n\n```go\n// 1. sentinel — matchable, no data\nvar ErrRateLimited = errors.New(\"rate limited\")\n\n// 2. typed — matchable AND data-carrying\ntype RateLimitError struct {\n    RetryAfter time.Duration\n}\nfunc (e *RateLimitError) Error() string {\n    return fmt.Sprintf(\"rate limited, retry after %v\", e.RetryAfter)\n}\n\n// caller\ntype target *RateLimitError\nif errors.As(err, &target) {\n    time.Sleep(target.RetryAfter)  // the data the caller needed\n    retry()\n}\n```\n\nerrors.As finds the type anywhere in the wrap chain and gives the caller the struct — with fields, not prose. The sentinel answered 'was it rate limiting?'; the type also answers 'for how long?'.\n\nSo the honest summary: errors.New is perfect when the identity is the whole story. The moment the caller's next action depends on data about the failure — and retry-backoff, redirect targets, and which-record questions all do — the error needs to be a type. Not a string that the caller regexes; a value with fields.",
   "Why is 'parse the message' so strongly forbidden? Because it couples the caller to your prose. The day the message becomes 'rate-limited, try in 30s' for tone, every string-matching caller silently breaks — no compile error, just a failed match at runtime that looks like a different error. Struct fields are a contract; strings are a convenience.\n\nThe deeper structure here: an error value's job is to let the caller branch correctly. Different branches need different information:\n\n- 'Is this retryable?' → a Retryable() bool method, or a sentinel set membership\n- 'What do I tell the user?' → a UserMessage() or typed codes\n- 'What do I log/alert?' → the wrapped chain already carries it\n- 'Which record failed?' → a field on the type\n\nerrors.New answers none of these beyond identity; fmt.Errorf with %w preserves the chain that does; a custom type with methods carries the answers directly. The standard library's os.PathError is the canonical middle step: it's a struct with Op, Path, and Err fields — a type, because 'which path failed' is the first thing every caller of file code asks. When your callers would ask a question the message can't answer structurally, you've outgrown errors.New."),
],

"multiple-return-error": [
  ("Why does Go return (value, error) pairs?",
   "Errors are values. The signature documents what can fail; the caller must handle it at the call site; multiple returns make the pairing natural.",
   "Go's founders looked at exception systems and rejected them for Go. Instead, an error is an ordinary value — usually the last return — and every caller decides, right at the call, what to do about it. The function signature itself tells you it can fail; there's no invisible throw path to go hunting for.\n\n```go\nf, err := os.Open(path)\nif err != nil {\n    return fmt.Errorf(\"load config: %w\", err)\n}\n```\n\nThat explicitness is the design. With exceptions, a call that can fail looks identical to one that can't — the failure exits through a side channel the compiler doesn't force you to acknowledge. With (value, error), the check is three lines you can see, and unused-variable rules mean you can't accidentally ignore the error entirely without the compiler noticing.\n\nThe cost is honest: Go code has more visible if err != nil branches than equivalent exception code has catch blocks. But the branches are where the handling actually lives, and their visibility is what keeps failure paths reviewed. You get to complain about the verbosity; you don't get surprise control flow.",
   "What makes the pattern work is a set of small language decisions acting together:\n\n1. Multiple return values — the pair is expressible at all.\n2. The error interface — one method, any type can implement it, so failures can be as rich as needed.\n3. Unused-variable checking — you can't get the value without at least acknowledging the error slot (using _ on it is an explicit choice, not an accident).\n4. No exceptions — there is no competing failure channel, so the visible one is the real one.\n\nThe contrast with exception languages isn't verbosity so much as where the knowledge lives. A Java caller of a throwing method must read the docs to know it throws; the compiler enforces catch-or-declare but not understanding. A Go caller sees the error in the signature and handles it or visibly discards it. The information moved from prose to types.\n\nThe payoff shows in code review: a reviewer can see every failure path of a Go function by reading it. With exceptions, the same review needs the whole call graph. That's the deep reason Go chose this — not terseness, but auditability of failure."),
  ("What are the conventions for the error return value?",
   "error is last; on failure, other values are zero/nil; on success, error is nil. Check err before using other values.",
   "The conventions are stable enough that the whole ecosystem can rely on them:\n\nThe error, when present, is the last return value. Not second-of-three or configurable — last. Every Go programmer reads (bytes, err) and (n int, err error) the same way because the position never varies.\n\nOn failure, the other return values should be their zero values — nil, 0, empty. This lets callers use one guard: check err first, and if it's non-nil, don't trust anything else. A function that returns a partial result AND an error splits the convention and forces callers to read docs. Don't do it: on error, return nil for the rest.\n\nOn success, error is nil. Not 'nil sometimes with a warning' — nil means the other values are valid.\n\nAnd the check order: err first, because reading other values before the check is the classic bug — a failed read may return a stale or zero count that the caller then acts on. The if err != nil guard at the top of the handling block isn't ceremony; it's the sequencing that keeps invalid data from flowing.",
   "Why do these conventions hold so strictly? Because they compose. Consider a caller three layers up:\n\n```go\nfunc loadUser(id string) (*User, error) {\n    row, err := db.Query(id)     // convention: err nil → row valid\n    if err != nil {\n        return nil, err           // convention: on failure, nil result\n    }\n    return row.toUser(), nil      // convention: success → nil error\n}\n```\n\nEvery function obeying the conventions makes the next one simpler: the caller's job is always check-then-use, wrap-then-return. One function breaking them — returning a usable value alongside a non-nil error — forces every caller to read its docs to know whether the value is usable. Conventions fail when they're convenient to break; these survive because breaking them taxes everyone downstream.\n\nThe zero-value-on-error rule has one deliberate exception in the standard library: io.Reader's Read returns the count of bytes actually read BEFORE the error (including io.EOF). That's documented, universal, and the one case where partial results plus error is the right contract — data is precious, so you keep what you got even as you report the failure. Note how the exception proves the design: it exists only where data loss would otherwise occur, and it's documented everywhere it applies."),
  ("Can a function return multiple errors?",
   "Idiomatically one. Go 1.20's errors.Join(err...) wraps several into a single error that errors.Is-matches each component.",
   "A Go function returns one error value — but that one value can represent several failures. Before Go 1.20 you handled this with fmt.Errorf and %w, which wraps exactly one cause; truly parallel failures (this batch item 3 failed AND item 7 failed) needed a custom multi-error type.\n\nGo 1.20 standardized it: errors.Join takes any number of errors and returns a single error that reports all of them, unwraps to each, and matches each with errors.Is. The joined error's message is the components' messages joined with newlines.\n\n```go\nvar errs []error\nfor _, t := range tasks {\n    if err := t.Run(); err != nil {\n        errs = append(errs, fmt.Errorf(\"task %s: %w\", t.Name, err))\n    }\n}\nif len(errs) > 0 {\n    return errors.Join(errs...)\n}\n```\n\nThe caller still sees ONE error from the function — the convention holds — but errors.Is can find any component cause, and the printed message includes every failure. The batch pattern (run everything, report everything that failed, not just the first) is the main use: fail-fast wastes the information about what else would have failed.\n\nBefore 1.20 and in code that must support older versions, the same shape is built by hand: a small struct with an Error() method that concatenates, and an Unwrap() []error method — which is exactly what Join provides for you now.",
   "Why was this added to the standard library in 2023 after a decade of one-error returns? Because the batch pattern had converged on private reimplementations. Everyone running parallel validation — form field checks, linters, CI steps, fan-out requests — hit the same wall: the first error is a subset of the truth, and swallowing the rest makes the caller iterate one-fix-one-round-trip. The multi-error structure shows up in enough places that the pattern deserved one shared implementation with correct Is/As semantics (a hand-rolled version usually gets the unwrap walk wrong).\n\nThe semantics that make Join correct: errors.Is(joinedErr, anyComponent) is true for each component; errors.As extracts each component type; the message joins all. The subtle part is nil handling — Join filters out nil components, and Join() with all-nils returns nil, so the builder pattern above doesn't need a nil guard.\n\nThe remaining guidance: one error return is still the default. Join is for genuinely parallel, independent failures. Sequential code (open file, parse it, save it) should still return the first error — the later steps didn't run, so reporting them as failures would be fiction."),
  ("What is the blank identifier's role with errors?",
   "_, err := f() takes only the error; v, _ := f() ignores the error. The second is a lint smell — you lose the failure signal silently.",
   "The blank identifier is how you opt out of one side of the (value, error) pair explicitly. `_, err := f()` says 'I need only the error' — common when you only care whether something succeeded. `v, _ := f()` says 'I need only the value, ignore the error' — and that second form deserves scrutiny.\n\nIgnoring an error is sometimes correct: a best-effort cache write, a metrics flush, a debug log where failure changes nothing. In those cases `_` documents the decision at the call site — a reader sees that the error was considered and deliberately dropped, not overlooked.\n\nBut most `v, _ :=` calls are bugs in waiting. The failure the error reported doesn't disappear; it moves downstream — the zero value gets used, and the real cause becomes a mystery later ('why is this config empty?', 'why did nothing get written?'). The compiler accepted it silently, no test caught it, and production is where it surfaces. That's why linters flag discarded errors by default: the pattern is legal but the justification should be local and visible.\n\nThe middle path: don't ignore, log. `v, err := f(); if err != nil { log.Printf(\"...: %v\", err) }` — three lines that convert a silent failure into a diagnosable one. Use `_` only where you can say, out loud, why the error doesn't matter.",
   "The mechanism is worth one layer down: the blank identifier is a write-only pseudo-variable. You can assign to it as many times as you like, read from it never. For the (value, error) contract, it's the explicit release valve — Go requires you to handle unused returns, and `_` is how you say 'unused on purpose.'\n\nThe interesting asymmetry: `_, err :=` is almost always fine (you want the error; the value being zero on failure is irrelevant), while `v, _ :=` is the one that hides bugs. The direction matters because the error is the signal and the value is the payload — discarding the payload is harmless when the signal says the payload is absent; discarding the signal means the payload's absence will surprise you.\n\nThe enforcement history explains the ecosystem's stance: Go's compiler checks unused VARIABLES but not unused return values in general — the single-return-value assignment would be an error, but multi-return with `_` never is. That gap between 'the language lets me' and 'the ecosystem expects' is exactly what errcheck-style linters filled. When a language leaves a discipline to convention, the convention grows teeth as tooling."),
  ("How does the (T, error) pattern affect API design?",
   "It forces explicit failure modeling: every call site answers 'what happens if this fails?' — visible branches instead of hidden throws.",
   "The pattern shapes APIs before any implementation exists. When you design a function that can fail, Go's signature forces the question: what does the caller get back on failure? A (T, error) return says 'you might get nothing but an explanation.' The signature is the contract, and it's impossible to add a failure mode later without changing it — every caller sees and adapts. Compare adding a new thrown exception in another language: existing callers find out when it flies past them at runtime.\n\nThis pushes toward a particular style of API: fewer things that 'sometimes fail silently,' more small operations with clear failure contracts. Constructors become pairs — NewX() (X, error) — and the zero-value-vs-validation debate (make invalid states unrepresentable vs a usable zero value) plays out in every design.\n\nIt also standardizes the caller's shape. Every consumer of a fallible API has the same skeleton — call, check, wrap-or-handle — so teams can read each other's failure paths fluently. The uniformity is the point: a Go codebase reads like one style of error handling, not N personal ones.",
   "The design pressure shows best at the boundaries. Consider a config loader:\n\n```go\n// Option A: panics on bad config — callers get crashes for input problems\n// Option B: returns (Config, error) — callers decide: exit, default, retry\nfunc Load(path string) (Config, error)\n```\n\nOption B is forced honesty: the API author had to decide which failures belong to the caller. That decision is API design. Option A offloads it to whoever hits the panic — the caller becomes the error handler by surprise, with less context than the library had.\n\nThe same forcing appears in constructors. A constructor that can fail must return (T, error) — so the type's zero value becomes a design question: is a zero Config usable (use a default) or invalid (require New)? Go's answer varies by type — usable zero values are idiomatic (bytes.Buffer works zero-constructed) where possible, and the (T, error) constructor exists for the cases where validation is mandatory. Neither escape hatch exists in exception-based APIs; the signature can't pose the question. That's the pattern's real gift to API design: the failure contract is a first-class, typed part of the interface, not folklore in the docs."),
],

"maps-make-and-literal": [
  ("How do you create a map in Go, and what happens if you write to a nil map?",
   "make(map[string]int) or a literal map[string]int{\"a\": 1}. A nil map reads fine (zero values) but writes panic: assignment to entry in nil map.",
   "Two working ways: the make form for runtime-populated maps, and the literal form when the initial contents are known at code time.\n\n```go\nm := make(map[string]int)          // empty, ready for writes\nm[\"count\"] = 3\n\ndefaults := map[string]int{        // starts with contents\n    \"retries\": 3,\n    \"timeout\": 30,\n}\n```\n\nAnd one broken way: declaring the var and writing before initializing.\n\n```go\nvar m map[string]int               // nil — header exists, no buckets\nm[\"k\"] = 1                          // panic: assignment to entry in nil map\n```\n\nThe asymmetry surprises people: reads on that same nil map work fine — a lookup returns the zero value, len is 0, range runs zero iterations. Only the write panics. The why: a map value is a pointer to a hash structure; nil points at nothing, so a write has nowhere to put the entry, while a read of 'nothing' can honestly answer 'not present, here's your zero value.'\n\nSo the rule is simple: make() (or a literal) before any write. Reads on nil are safe; the panic is write-only, and it's the most common map runtime error in beginner Go.",
   "Why the write-only panic? Look at what the two operations need:\n\nA read asks 'is there an entry with key k, and what's its value?' On a nil map, the honest answer is 'no such entry' — which the runtime expresses as (zero value, not-present). No structure needed; absence is expressible without storage.\n\nA write asks 'put this entry somewhere findable.' That needs a bucket array, hash seeds, growth machinery — the whole map structure that make() allocates. With a nil pointer to that structure, there's nowhere to put it. Panic is the runtime refusing to pretend.\n\nThe comparison with slices clarifies the design: a nil slice CAN be appended to, because append's allocate-path creates the backing storage on demand — a slice header alone is enough to start. A map can't be lazily created because the map value is a pointer to an ALREADY-COMPLETE structure; there's no 'grow into it' path the way a slice header grows into its array. So var s []int; s = append(s, x) is fine, var m map[string]int; m[x] = 1 panics — the asymmetry is structural, not arbitrary.\n\nThe bug pattern it creates: passing a nil map into a function that populates it. The fix is return-the-map (the function makes and returns it) rather than fill-the-parameter — which is also just cleaner Go."),
  ("make(map) with capacity hint — what does it do?",
   "make(map[K]V, n) pre-allocates roughly n entries' worth of buckets. A performance hint only — the map grows past n without error.",
   "The size argument in make(map[K]V, n) is a capacity hint, not a limit. It tells the runtime 'I expect about n entries — size the initial bucket structure for that,' avoiding the early growth-and-rehash cycles as the map fills from zero.\n\nThe hint is purely advisory. Exceed it and the map grows normally — no error, no truncation, no visible difference except allocation timing. Undershoot it (hint 1000, use 10) and you've wasted some memory up front; the runtime doesn't refund it.\n\nWhen it matters: maps that will definitely hold a known-count set — building a lookup from a slice of length n (hint n), a count-by-key aggregation over known input. For small maps or unknown sizes, the hint buys nothing measurable; the default growth handles small maps in one allocation anyway.\n\nAnd note the contrast with slices: make([]T, n) on a slice makes n ELEMENTS (zeroed, len n). make(map, n) makes capacity for n entries but zero entries exist. The same function name, two different size semantics — the slice's number is a length, the map's is a hint.",
   "What the hint controls is the initial bucket count. A Go map is a hash table: an array of buckets, each holding ~8 entries, with overflow chaining and incremental rehashing on growth. From empty, the first inserts trigger several grow-rehash steps as the entry count crosses thresholds — cheap individually, but a loop inserting 100k entries into an unhinted map pays for every growth along the way.\n\nmake(map[string]int, 100_000) pre-sizes the bucket array for ~100k entries — the growth steps happen once at allocation instead of during the fill loop. For big, size-knowable builds that's a real win; benchmarks show it clearly at six-figure sizes.\n\nThe '~' in 'roughly n buckets' matters: the runtime computes the bucket count from n via its own formula (n/8 buckets, rounded per growth class), so hint 100 doesn't allocate exactly 100 slots' worth — it allocates the size class that fits. That's fine; the hint is an input to a heuristic, not an exact reservation.\n\nThe debugging lesson it encodes: map performance issues are usually allocation/rehash churn, visible in profiling as runtime.makemap and growth allocations. When a profile shows that, the hint is the fix; when it doesn't, adding hints is noise."),
  ("Map literal vs make — when each?",
   "Literal when initial pairs are static: map[string]int{\"retries\": 3}. make when populated at runtime or when you want the capacity hint.",
   "The literal form declares contents in the code: good for defaults, fixed lookups, enums-as-maps, test fixtures. The make form starts empty and is filled at runtime: good for accumulators, caches, parsed-config, anything whose keys come from data.\n\n```go\n// literal — the contents ARE the source of truth\nstatusText := map[int]string{\n    200: \"OK\",\n    404: \"Not Found\",\n    500: \"Internal Server Error\",\n}\n\n// make — contents arrive at runtime\nwordCount := make(map[string]int)\nfor _, w := range strings.Fields(text) {\n    wordCount[w]++\n}\n```\n\nThe literal has one extra behavior worth knowing: it's also valid as an empty initializer, map[string]int{} — a non-nil empty map, safe to write immediately. That's the quick fix for the nil-map-write panic: `m := map[string]int{}` instead of `var m map[string]int` when you need write-ready.\n\nThe decision is rarely contested: static content in code → literal; dynamic content → make (with a size hint when the eventual size is knowable). The smell to avoid: a giant literal that mirrors what should be data (a config file, a table) — a 50-entry literal of values that change operationally is config wearing a map's clothes.",
   "The two forms differ in when their contents exist — and that decides everything else. A literal is fully constructed before any code runs: keys hashed, buckets laid out, entries stored, at program init for package-level literals. That's why fixed lookups (status codes, default configs, enum-to-string tables) are literals — the data is as static as the code, and initializing it once at startup is free.\n\nmake() constructs the same machinery but empty: buckets for zero-or-hinted entries, filled by whatever the program discovers at runtime. The contents are data-driven — parsed input, counted events, cached results — so they can't be a literal even if you wanted them to be.\n\nThe empty literal map[string]int{} is the bridge between the forms: a literal with zero entries, fully initialized, immediately writable. It's the idiomatic answer to 'I need a write-ready map and have nothing to prefill' — and its existence is why the nil-map panic is always a var-declaration mistake, never a necessity: write-ready is one {} away.\n\nThe style signal to read in code review: literals whose values change operationally (feature flags updated at runtime, limits read from config files) are config that outgrew its form — the moment values change without code changes, they belong in loaded data, and the literal is the migration debt."),
  ("What are the zero values in a Go map?",
   "A missing key returns the VALUE TYPE's zero value: m[\"missing\"] is 0, \"\", or false — not an error. The comma-ok idiom distinguishes stored-zero from missing.",
   "Map lookups never fail on a missing key — they return the zero value of the value type. A missing int key gives 0; a missing string gives \"\"; a missing bool gives false; a missing struct value gives the struct with all fields zeroed. The two-valued lookup distinguishes present from absent:\n\n```go\nv := m[\"maybe\"]            // 0 if absent, or if stored 0 — ambiguous\nv, ok := m[\"maybe\"]       // ok: false → absent; true → present (even if v is 0)\n```\n\nThe single-value form is a feature for counting and accumulating — m[w]++ works whether w exists (increments) or not (0+1=1) — but it's a trap for presence-sensitive logic: checking 'did they opt in?' via `if m[optin]` breaks when false is a valid stored answer.\n\nThe comma-ok form is the precise instrument: it answers presence and value as separate facts. Use it whenever a stored zero value is legitimate data — optional fields, flags that start false, sparse counters at 0.\n\nThe interface to internalize: a map lookup is total — every key gives an answer. The zero value isn't an error signal; it's just the default. When you need to distinguish 'default because absent' from 'explicitly the default,' that's what ok is for.",
   "The zero-value-on-miss design is what makes Go's accumulate patterns one-liners:\n\n```go\n// count words — works whether w is new or repeated\nfor _, w := range words {\n    counts[w]++        // absent: 0+1; present: n+1\n}\n\n// group by key — same trick with append\nfor _, item := range items {\n    groups[item.Kind] = append(groups[item.Kind], item)  // absent: nil+one = [item]\n}\n```\n\nThe nil-slice zero value makes the second pattern work — appending to a nil slice is valid, so the absent case starts from nil and grows. Two zero-value rules (map miss → zero; nil slice → appendable) compose into idiomatic grouping that needs no existence checks.\n\nThe design choice here is total vs partial functions. Most languages make lookup partial — it throws or returns a sentinel — and every caller writes the guard. Go makes it total — every key answers — and reserves the comma-ok form for when presence itself is the question. The cost is that 'missing' and 'zero' collapse into one value on the fast path; the benefit is that the 80% of map use that treats 'default' as a valid answer needs no ceremony."),
  ("How do map keys work — what types can be keys?",
   "Any comparable type: strings, ints, bools, arrays, structs of comparables, pointers, interfaces. NOT slices, maps, or functions — they have no ==.",
   "A key type must support equality, because a hash table needs to (1) hash the key and (2) compare a probed key against the stored one. Anything with == defined can be a key: strings, all numeric types, bools, pointers, channels, interfaces, arrays, and structs whose fields are all comparable.\n\nWhat can't: slices, maps, and functions. == is not defined for them (comparing two slices can't answer 'same contents?' cheaply and Go refuses reference-vs-content ambiguity), so they can't hash or match. A struct containing a slice field inherits the restriction — no == for the struct, no key.\n\nThe practically valuable cases beyond strings and ints:\n\n```go\n// struct keys — composite lookups\nkey := struct{ Region, AZ string }{\"us-east\", \"1a\"}\ncounts[key]++\n\n// array keys — fixed-size sequences\nvar ip [4]byte\ncopy(ip[:], bytes)\nseen[ip] = true\n```\n\nStruct keys are the underrated one: a small comparable struct gives you multi-field lookup keys without string-concatenation or nested maps. The rule is just the == rule — and the compile error for a bad key type (invalid map key type) is the language teaching it at build time.",
   "Why is == the gate? Trace the hash-table operations:\n\ninsert: hash(k) → bucket → store (k, v). The key must hash stably.\nlookup: hash(k) → bucket → compare k against stored keys with ==. Equality must match hash equality.\n\nBoth operations lean on the same two properties: a stable hash and a well-defined equality that agrees with it. Slices break both: contents mutate (hash rots) and == between two slice headers checks pointer+len — not the semantics a lookup wants. Go's designers chose to exclude rather than pick a semantics (deep-equal keys would need O(n) compares per probe and a defined deep-hash) — the constraint pushes you toward strings, ints, and structs, which cover real key shapes honestly.\n\nThe mutability subtlety for struct keys: == compares FIELDS, so two struct keys with equal fields are the same key — value semantics. But if the struct contains a POINTER field, == compares the pointer, and two structs pointing at equal-but-distinct data are different keys. The key's behavior is exactly its =='s behavior — no more, no less. When that surprises, the fix is storing the data you match on (the dereferenced values) in the key, not the pointer."),
],
}
